  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... \nAs the stationary front continues to sag south, a ridge will \nbegin to build through the remainder of today and into the \nweekend. An embedded shortwave within this pattern will promote \nmoisture transport into the area tonight and into Friday. The \nlatest model runs show the best chance for precipitation along \nand south of the 30 corridor, with the highest PoPs in Marion, \nMorrow, and Knox counties Friday morning. There is a chance for \nan embedded thunderstorm or two, though mainly rain showers are\nexpected. Additional rainfall amounts should remain below one-\nhalf inch.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nThe next period for unsettled weather will be Saturday night \nthrough Sunday as a low pressure system moves east through the \nGreat Lakes, extending a cold front across the area Sunday \nnight. A period of heavy rain is becoming more likely Saturday\nnight as a highly-anoamlous moist air mass, characterized by \nPWATS exceeding 2.0 inches, enters the area. The latest \nguidance appears to be trending the strong to severe storm\nthreat further south and southeast of the area as any\nredevelopment in the afternoon will likely focus along the\nresidual boundary from the overnight convection.",
